Project: Super-Drone (Prototype)

Overview
- Small prototype drone using an ESP32-based flight computer. Initial prototype implements basic sensor fusion and altitude estimation. Designed for manual assembly (soldered components, 3D-printed frame) and future upgrades (motors, cameras, advanced flight components).

Goals
- Demonstrate stable hover and basic attitude control using onboard sensors and a PID controller.
- Provide a minimal, extensible software and hardware baseline for future expansion.

Scope (Prototype)
- Hardware: ESP32 flight computer, MPU6050 IMU, BME280 barometric/temperature/humidity sensor, small motors, propellers, 3D printed frame; manual soldering and wiring.
- Software: Sensor acquisition, simple sensor fusion (IMU + barometer), altitude estimation, PID-based attitude/altitude controller, servo control, basic abort/emergency stop routine.

Hardware List (Minimum for Prototype)
- Flight computer: ESP32 (development module or equivalent)
- IMU: MPU6050 (accelerometer + gyroscope)
- Barometer / environmental sensor: BME280 (pressure → altitude, temperature, humidity)
- Actuators: Small brushless/DC motors (prototype-appropriate), ESCs or driver circuits, or micro servos for control surfaces if applicable
- Propellers sized to motors
- Power: Battery (LiPo recommended) and power distribution (regulators as needed)
- Frame: 3D-printed structural parts, mounts, and fasteners
- Assembly: Hand-soldered connections, wires, connectors, heatshrink
- Safety: Propeller guards (recommended for testing), kill-switch or accessible power disconnect

Software List (Minimum for Prototype)
- Flight stack: Firmware running on `ESP32` that:
	- Reads raw sensor data from `MPU6050` and `BME280` over I2C
	- Runs a basic sensor-fusion algorithm (complementary filter or simple Kalman) for attitude estimation
	- Estimates altitude using barometric data and optionally fused IMU integration
	- Implements PID controllers for roll, pitch, yaw and altitude
	- Provides actuator output to motors/servos (PWM/ESC protocols)
	- Implements an emergency abort/kill routine that stops motors safely
- Utilities: I2C scanner and sensor test sketches, logging/debug output over serial
- Development: Build scripts and instructions for flashing onto ESP32, basic telemetry/serial monitor

Functional Requirements
- FR1: The system shall sample IMU (MPU6050) and barometer (BME280) at rates sufficient for closed-loop control (IMU ≥ 200 Hz recommended; barometer as available).
- FR2: The system shall compute roll, pitch, and yaw using sensor fusion.
- FR3: The system shall estimate altitude from barometer (and optionally IMU) and provide altitude feedback to the controller.
- FR4: The system shall run PID loops controlling attitude and altitude with tunable gains.
- FR5: The system shall command motor/servo outputs and provide a safe stop (abort) mechanism.
- FR6: The system shall provide serial telemetry for debugging (sensor readings, estimated states, controller outputs).

Non-Functional Requirements
- NFR1: Firmware must fit and run reliably on ESP32 with available RAM/flash for the prototype features.
- NFR2: System must be testable on a bench with props removed and in a restrained test rig before free flight.
- NFR3: Documentation must include wiring diagrams, pin mappings, build/flash steps, and basic tuning guidance for PID gains.

Constraints and Assumptions
- Prototype will be manually soldered and assembled; reliability expectations are limited compared to production assemblies.
- No autopilot-level navigation is required for initial prototype — only basic attitude/altitude control.
- Communications beyond serial (e.g., RC link, telemetry radios) are optional for first-pass prototype.

Safety and Abort
- An accessible hard power disconnect and software abort (motor shutoff) must be available.
- During testing, propellers should be removed or the vehicle restrained until basic control is verified.

Testing and Validation
- Unit tests: sensor readout validation (I2C scanner, MPU6050/BME280 validation sketches).
- Integration tests: sensor fusion correctness (compare IMU-derived attitude vs expected orientations), PID response tests on a test rig.
- Flight test plan: tethered hover tests, short elevation bursts, PID tuning iterations; log results.

Simulation
- Purpose: validate dynamics, sensor models, and controller behavior before physical assembly; shorten iteration loop and reduce risk.
- Recommended tools: MATLAB/Simulink (Aerospace/Simulink toolboxes) for control-focused simulation; Gazebo/ROS or PX4 SITL for full-stack and HIL-style tests; Python (NumPy/SciPy) for lightweight dynamic prototypes.
Project: Super-Drone (Prototype)

Goals & Basic Requirements
- Goals:
	- Achieve stable hover and basic attitude control (roll, pitch, yaw) with PID control.
	- Provide a minimal, extensible baseline for future upgrades (motors, cameras, autonomy).
- Basic requirements:
	- Read IMU and barometer sensors and run sensor fusion for attitude and altitude estimation.
	- Run PID controllers for attitude and altitude with tunable gains and telemetry output.
	- Provide safe abort (software kill) and bench/test modes before free flight.

Hardware
- Core components (prototype minimum):
	- Flight computer: ESP32 development module.
	- IMU: MPU6050 (accelerometer + gyroscope) on I2C.
	- Barometric sensor: BME280 (pressure/temperature/humidity) on I2C for altitude estimation.
	- Actuators: small motors + ESCs or micro servos (depending on frame), propellers sized to motors.
	- Power: LiPo battery, power distribution, and required regulators.
	- Frame: 3D-printed parts, mounts, fasteners; hand-soldered wiring and connectors.
	- Safety: accessible power disconnect, kill-switch, prop guards for bench testing.
- Hardware notes:
	- Keep wiring and grounding tidy; use common ground and decoupling near power inputs.
	- Provide clearly documented pin mapping for ESP32 I2C, PWM, and power pins.

Software
- Required features:
	- Sensor drivers: robust I2C routines for `MPU6050` and `BME280` with error handling and simple calibration routines.
	- Sensor fusion: lightweight complementary filter or simple Kalman/EKF for roll/pitch/yaw estimation.
	- Altitude estimation: barometer-based altitude with optional short-term IMU fusion.
	- Control: PID controllers for roll, pitch, yaw, and altitude with gain parameters and runtime tuning hooks.
	- Actuation: PWM/ESC output layer and safe motor/servo arming/disarming sequence.
	- Safety: software abort/kill and bench mode (motors disabled) for testing.
	- Telemetry & debug: serial logging of sensor values, estimated states, and controller outputs; optional simple RC/telemetry integration later.
- Performance expectations:
	- IMU loop: target reading & control update at ~200 Hz (or as hardware permits).
	- Barometer: lower-rate updates (e.g., 25–50 Hz) fused into altitude estimator.
- Development utilities:
	- I2C scanner and individual sensor test sketches.
	- Flashing instructions and a simple serial monitor setup for telemetry.

Simulation
- Purpose: validate dynamics, sensor models, and controller behavior before building hardware.
- Recommended tools and approaches:
	- MATLAB/Simulink for control-design and rapid PID tuning.
	- Gazebo/ROS or PX4 SITL for system-level testing (optional, heavier setup).
	- Python (NumPy/SciPy) notebooks for small dynamics experiments and Monte Carlo tests.
- Minimal simulation models to implement:
	- 6-DOF rigid-body dynamics (mass, inertias) and simple motor thrust models.
	- Sensor models approximating `MPU6050` noise/bias and `BME280` pressure noise/update rate.
	- Disturbances: gravity, simple wind/gust models for robustness checks.
- Workflows and deliverables:
	- Controller-in-the-loop: run the PID controller against the dynamics model to generate initial gain values.
	- Parameter sweeps: vary mass, CG, sensor noise, and latency to check robustness and failure cases.
	- Deliverables: model scripts or Simulink files, initial PID gain suggestions, and plots showing attitude/altitude responses.
- MATLAB quick notes:
	- Use a fixed-step solver matched to controller rate (e.g., 1/200 s).
	- Implement sensor sampling/aliasing and simple bias/noise models to get realistic data for controller tuning.

Minimal Acceptance Criteria
- Bench: sensors report plausible values and fusion produces stable attitude output while stationary.
- Bench: PID loops run in bench mode without motors and produce stable controller outputs.
- Tethered test: vehicle responds predictably to command inputs and can maintain brief, controlled hover under supervision.

